# M5 red team, finding docs/redteam/m5-movss-db.md: a ring-3 thread sets
# TF with popfq, then runs `mov ss, eax` and a control transfer into the
# kernel: syscall, int3, int 0x80, int1 or ud2. MOV SS defers the single
# step past the next instruction (SDM vol. 3A, 6.8.3; CVE-2018-8897), so on
# hardware, and on Hyper-V, which runs guest code on the CPU, the #DB after
# int3 arrives in ring 0 at the first instruction of the #BP stub. The
# kernel panicked on it ("fatal trap") until the M5 fix, which returns from
# it; any other ring-0 #DB still panics. QEMU 8.2 TCG does not defer it: it
# raises the #DB in ring 3 right after `mov ss`, so this scenario passes
# here either way. The M5 Hyper-V check boots it on a real CPU
# (tools/hyperv/README.md).
cmdline: test=redteam-movss
exit: success
expect: microL4 kernel
expect: root: cmdline "test=redteam-movss"
expect: redteam: movss: 5 probes in thread
expect: probe 0 (mov ss; syscall (Yield) with TF): label 3, 0x1/0x0
expect: probe 1 (mov ss; int3 with TF): label 3, 0x1/0x0
expect: probe 2 (mov ss; int 0x80 with TF): label 3, 0x1/0x0
expect: redteam: movss: all 5 probes ended in their own fault
expect: TEST PASS
expect: DEBUG HALT 0 from TCB
